Date: Fri, 14 May 93 07:59:50 PDT
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: TECH: Numerical Speculation

Robin Hanson writes:
> 
> As you might expect, I think it would be great to form consensus on
> such estimates through betting markets.  Such markets could create
> probability distributions over such parameters (eg # alien civils,
> bits/brain).  If you thought the consensus was too certain, you could
> buy assets near the tails of the distribution.  Thus a social process
> could correct for overconfidence, and still return numbers, even under
> very high uncertainty.  

Actually, I don't quite see how this would work.  Suppose we were running
a betting market on the question, "are there other industrial civilizations
in the galaxy?"  You could buy "yes" and "no" tickets, and their relative
prices could be used to impute a probability estimate to the question.  But
what kind of side markets would you run to give an indication of how
precise this estimate is?

Hal

