Date: Fri, 14 May 93 09:41:14 PDT
From: GRAPS@galileo.arc.nasa.gov
Subject: Drake Equation and Modelling of the Unknown

Hal Finney says:

>The Drake equation (could someone post it here, with a list of
>the factors?) has a lot of implicit assumptions. 

Here it is:

N = R* x fp x ne  x fl x fi  x fc x L
   ^^^^^^^^^^^^^    ^^^^^^^    ^^^^^^^
     physical       biological  cultural


N = the number of advanced communicating civilizations in our galaxy
R* = the rate at which stars are formed in our galaxy per year
fp = the fraction of stars, once formed, that will have a planetary system
ne = the number of planets in each planetary system that will have an 
      environment suitable for life
fl = the probability that life will develop on a suitable planet
fi = the probability that life will evolve to an intelligent state
fc = the probability that intelligent life will develop a culture
     capable of communication over interstellar distances
L = the time (in years) that such a culture will spend actually
    trying to communicate


A very good discussion of this equation can be found in chapter 6, titled
"Where are They?" in _Paradigms Lost_ by John Casti. He works through each 
of these 7 terms and assigns probabilities and ranges of numbers and describes
experiments that scientists are conducting/have conducted to try to come up
with better numbers.

Aside: 
This book could be used as a springboard for new extropians on this list who 
want a source for information on many of the basic topics debated here. Some 
other chapters are "Faith, Hope, and Asperity" (belief systems, science and 
the  invention of reality), "It's in the Genes" (investigating the claim that 
human  behavior patterns are dictated primarily by the genes), "Speaking for 
Myself" (investing the claim that human language capacity stems from a unique 
innate property of the brain), "The Cognitive Engine" (investigating the claim 
that digital computers can, in principle, literally think), "How Real is the 
Real World" (investigating the claim that there exists no objective reality 
independent of an observer)  I'd like to write a review of this book as soon 
as I finish reading it...

Another aside:
The Drake equation poses one of the great multi-disciplinary problems of all
time. I think it can be effectively used by teachers to bring in several
important branches of science and show how they inter-relate with each other.


