Date: Fri, 14 May 93 13:18:30 -0400
From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Subject: AI: Speculations

I agree with Mark Muhlestein and others that this thread is very interesting,
especially Derek's contributions to it.  My point was only to say that
calculations of brain storage capacity are not meaningful because many of the
terms are largely unknown.  Hal Finney paraphrased my position very well in his
posts.

Derek said that few people really believe that current neural models are
accurate enough for discussion of uploading.  That's good, although judging
by the willingness of people to extrapolate upon storage requirements and
analogies to human phenomena (like deja-vu), it seems that many people do
believe this.  Such speculation goes on all over the place, not just on this
list.  Speculation is fine, of course... so is criticising it.

As you might expect, I would classify myself as a "type 2 NN person" - someone
who wants to model biological brains, as opposed to developing novel solutions
to pattern recognition etc.  Most people are not aware of the large difference
between these two NN approaches, and lumping them together apply results
from pattern recognition and curve-fitting techniques to brain phenomena.
Of course, to a neurophysiologist, the kind of assumptions I make are pretty
distasteful also.  It's all a matter of which level of analysis you want to
work on.  On one extreme we have the AI types who want to describe intelligence
as an abstract phenomenon, unrelated to biology.  On the other we have the
neurophysicists who practice extreme reductionism and argue that it's the
molecules we should be looking at.  I have nothing better to defend my position
than to say that it's the one I'm comfortable with - analysing the functional
properties of networks constrained by biological facts, and trying to answer
the question: "what is the nervous system doing?".

As such I do not believe that a purely thinking machine without sensory or
motor systems can be constructed that has any relation to human thought.  Our
conscious mind, rationality, and language, may be the things we are most
interested in, but this does not mean that they can be seperated from the
"simple" psychological phenomena that are their foundation...  But let's leave
this particular speculation to a later time...

-Paul


