Here's a quarter-baked idea:

We've done some work here with shape-memory alloys (Nitinol being
an example), with the purpose of designing Lunar micro-robots.
The alloy has the property that when you heat it above a transition
temperature, it can get shorter in one direction.  Then when cooled,
it returns to it's original shape.

To make a printing device, imagine a drum with Nitinol wires sticking
out radially, at a spacing equal to the print resolution you want.
On the inside is a row of electrical contacts, and towards the
outside surface is another row of electrical contacts.  As the drom
rotates, the contacts complete circuits through the wires, which
heat up beyond the transition temperature, and get shorter.  This
makes your print pattern.  Now the drum rotates and is inked using
conventional printing ink and paper.  When the requisite number of
copies is made, the drum is chilled (a blast of air conditioning?,
a chiller coil?), to prepare it for the next print.  Nitinol is
approximately a 50/50 alloy of Nickel and Titianium, and is on
the order of the strength of steel.  It is available in 3 mil
wires, which would give you 300 dpi resolution.

For one-off printing, the drum might rotate through electrical
contacts, inking, paper, and chiller on a continuous basis.  If the
mechanism could be made cheap enough, then ganging printers in
parallel would allow fairly high print speeds.

To exapand on this latter idea:

Take 100 inkjet printers (at say $400 each), for a capital investment
of $40,000.  Have them each print a different page of a book in
parallel, then collate the output by automated means.  You should be
able to print an entire book in a minute or two on demand this way.

Dani Eder


