I wrote:
>I think it would be great to form consensus on such estimates through
>betting markets.  Such markets could create probability distributions
>over such parameters (eg # alien civils, bits/brain).

Hal Finney writes:
>I think the problem I see in these difficult problems with wide error
>bars is that we really don't have enough information to form a decent
>estimate.  

In this and another post you seem to imply that there is some
information threshold below which you can't make any numerical or
probability estimates, even though you are not totally ignorant.  I
disagree.  You can always assign probabilities; they just aren't very
good if you don't know much.

>There is a paradox in probability theory that applies here ...
>I take two coins and lay them on the table either heads or tails,
>choosing how each will lie.  I then cover them, and we run a betting
>market on whether each coin is heads or tails, as well as the total
>number of heads exposed.  Later I will reveal the coins and we will
>settle the betting.
>
>What will the market do in this situation?  Equal odds of 50-50 for each
>coin?  What about the number of heads?  If we assumed independence the
>odds of 1 head are twice that of 0 or 2.  But that's not a valid
>assumption.  So are 0, 1, and 2 equally probable?  How does that square
>with the 50-50 odds for each coin?  How should a rational person bet?

Where is the paradox, with two conflicting but compelling answers?
Different "rational" people might have different beliefs, depending on
how well they knew you, and what they think they would do in your
place.  If someone subsidized it, there *would* be a market odds, and
I claim that odds would be as good, on average, as any competing
estimate made using similar information and resources.

>Actually, I don't quite see how this would work.  Suppose we were running
>a betting market on the question, "are there other industrial civilizations
>in the galaxy?"  You could buy "yes" and "no" tickets, and their relative
>prices could be used to impute a probability estimate to the question.  But
>what kind of side markets would you run to give an indication of how
>precise this estimate is?

I thought the question was how many industrial civilizations there are
in the galaxy.  $1 could be split into "$1 if 1 civ", "$1 if 2-10
civs", "$1 if 11-100 civs", ... "$1 if 10,000 - 100,000 civs" ...
"$1 if > trillion civs".  The market price for each asset would tell
the consensus probability that there are that many civs in our galaxy.

