Tim May writes:
>
>1. Around 1970, I read in "Scientific American" about an interesting
>experiment in eidetic memory. A man was shown one half of a random-dot
>stereogram, consisting of a milllion dots or so. This was just for a
>few seconds. Then he was shown another one. He was asked to mentally
>fuse the two images. He did so and discovered the "message" (some
>shape or symbol, I can't recall the details, ironically). The few
>million dots were thus remembered, if this is true, for at least a few
>minutes.
>

The conclusion "the million dots were remembered" is not justified. 
The subject was able to recreate enough of the features of the
original image to fuse it with the new image to see the underlying
message. That is all. The original million dots were probably highly
redundant with respect to the information needed to reconstruct
the image sufficiently to successfully fuse it with the new image.

